WEBVTT

00:00:00.028 --> 00:00:03.472
Three common tools help organize query results.

00:00:03.472 --> 00:00:06.162
ORDER BY controls the row order.

00:00:06.162 --> 00:00:09.647
LIMIT keeps only a chosen number of result rows.

00:00:09.647 --> 00:00:12.712
DISTINCT removes repeated result rows.

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:18.807
The important point is that LIMIT by itself does not mean latest, oldest, highest, or best.

00:00:18.807 --> 00:00:26.502
Without a deterministic ORDER BY, PostgreSQL is free to return any qualifying subset.

00:00:26.502 --> 00:00:30.832
Run the example that asks for the three most recent admissions.

00:00:30.832 --> 00:00:35.697
Notice that the date is sorted in descending order before the limit is applied.

00:00:35.697 --> 00:00:38.512
Now imagine removing ORDER BY.

00:00:38.512 --> 00:00:45.497
You may still get three rows, but you would no longer have a reliable reason to call them

00:00:45.497 --> 00:00:51.612
the three most recent. Use sorting to define what top or recent actually means before applying a limit.

00:00:51.612 --> 00:00:55.917
Think of SELECT work as progressively shaping a question.

00:00:55.917 --> 00:01:04.382
First identify the rows and columns you need, then filter, derive expressions, sort, and limit for inspection.

00:01:04.382 --> 00:01:09.977
A useful habit is to test each part on a small result before adding more complexity,

00:01:09.977 --> 00:01:13.356
especially when a filter contains NULL-sensitive logic.
